Day 14 – Ingress 🌐
1. Why Ingress?

Suppose we have two applications running in Kubernetes:

App 1 → Pod 1
App 2 → Pod 2

We need to allow users outside the cluster to access these applications.

One option is to expose each application separately:

App 1 → NodePort
App 2 → NodePort

For many applications, this becomes difficult to manage.

Instead, we can use Ingress as a single entry point.

                 One Entry Point
                       ↓
                    Ingress
                   /       \
                  ↓         ↓
                App 1      App 2

For example:

app1.example.com → App 1

app2.example.com → App 2

So the same external entry point can be used to reach different applications.

Remember:

Ingress = a way to manage external HTTP/HTTPS traffic and route it to different Services.

2. Ingress Controller

An important thing to understand:

Ingress itself does not actually process the traffic.

Ingress contains the routing rules.

Something needs to read those rules and actually route the requests.

That component is the Ingress Controller.

Think:

Ingress
   ↓
Defines rules

Ingress Controller
   ↓
Implements the rules

Example:

Ingress rule:

app1.example.com → app1-service

The Ingress Controller receives the request and routes it to:

app1-service
      ↓
   App 1 Pod
Simple definition:

Ingress Controller is the component that receives external traffic and implements the routing rules defined by Ingress.

Examples you may hear about:

NGINX Ingress Controller
Traefik
Cilium Ingress Controller

For Day 14, you don't need to learn their differences.

Remember:

Ingress = rules
Ingress Controller = implements the rules

3. Routing

Ingress can route traffic based on the hostname.

Suppose we have:

app1.example.com
app2.example.com

The Ingress can contain:

app1.example.com → app1-service

app2.example.com → app2-service

When the user requests:

https://app1.example.com

the hostname is:

app1.example.com

The Ingress Controller checks the hostname and routes the request:

app1.example.com
       ↓
Ingress Controller
       ↓
app1-service
       ↓
App 1 Pod

For App 2:

app2.example.com
       ↓
Ingress Controller
       ↓
app2-service
       ↓
App 2 Pod
Important idea:

One external entry point can route traffic to different applications based on the hostname.

4. Ingress Routing Flow

This is the overall picture:

                    User
                      |
                      ↓
              External Entry Point
                      |
                      ↓
              Ingress Controller
                      |
              ┌───────┴───────┐
              ↓               ↓
       app1.example.com   app2.example.com
              ↓               ↓
        app1-service      app2-service
              ↓               ↓
           App 1 Pod       App 2 Pod

Think of the Ingress Controller as a traffic director.

It looks at the incoming request and asks:

"Which hostname did the user request?"

Then it sends the request to the appropriate Service.

5. Hands-on — Route Two Applications Through One Entry Point

We created two applications.

App 1

Deployment:

nginx-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: app1
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: app1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: app1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: app1
        image: nginx

Apply:

kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml

Output:

deployment.apps/app1 created
App 1 Service
app1-service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: app1-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: app1
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80

Apply:

kubectl apply -f app1-service.yaml

Output:

service/app1-service created
Service connection

Remember:

Pod label:

app: app1

Service:

selector:
  app: app1

Therefore:

Service
   ↓
selector app=app1
   ↓
Finds App1 Pod
6. App 2

We created another Deployment:

nginx-deployment2.yaml

with:

app: app2

Then we created:

app2-service.yaml

with:

selector:
  app: app2

So now:

app1-service → App 1 Pod

app2-service → App 2 Pod

We checked:

kubectl get svc

and got:

NAME           TYPE        CLUSTER-IP       PORT(S)

app1-service   ClusterIP   10.98.55.205     80/TCP
app2-service   ClusterIP   10.102.208.108   80/TCP
What does ClusterIP mean?

ClusterIP is the default Service type.

It provides an internal Service IP inside the Kubernetes cluster.

For our architecture:

Ingress
   ↓
ClusterIP Service
   ↓
Pod

We don't need to expose each application directly.

7. Create Ingress

We created:

ingress.yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: my-ingress
spec:
  rules:
  - host: app1.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: app1-service
            port:
              number: 80

  - host: app2.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: app2-service
            port:
              number: 80

Apply:

kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml

Output:

ingress.networking.k8s.io/my-ingress created
8. Understanding Our Ingress YAML
First rule
host: app1.example.com

means:

app1.example.com
       ↓
app1-service
       ↓
App 1 Pod
Second rule
host: app2.example.com

means:

app2.example.com
       ↓
app2-service
       ↓
App 2 Pod

Therefore:

app1.example.com → app1-service → App1

app2.example.com → app2-service → App2
9. Check Ingress

Command:

kubectl get ingress

We got:

NAME         CLASS    HOSTS
my-ingress   <none>   app1.example.com,app2.example.com
Important columns

NAME

my-ingress

Name of our Ingress resource.

CLASS

<none>

No IngressClass was configured in our Killercoda cluster.

HOSTS

app1.example.com,app2.example.com

These are the hostnames for our routing rules.

ADDRESS

This was blank in our environment because there was no configured Ingress Controller providing an external address.

10. Why We Couldn't Fully Test the External Routing

This is important for our Killercoda hands-on.

We successfully created:

App 1                 ✅
App 2                 ✅
Services              ✅
Ingress rules         ✅

But the Killercoda environment we were using did not have an Ingress Controller configured to process these rules.

We checked:

kubectl get ingressclass

and got:

No resources found

Therefore, our Ingress resource existed, but there was no configured controller to actually serve the external requests.

Don't confuse these two:
Ingress resource
      ↓
Stores routing rules

versus:

Ingress Controller
      ↓
Actually handles traffic

For this learning exercise, we did not install or modify Cilium/another controller just to force the demo to work.

